>> A2: In a few paragraphs, describe your code for accessing the data
>> stored in the SPT about a given page.

SPT is a per-process data struct maps vitual pages for their physical frame
or storage location. it use page_map for efficient search, insert and delete.
each entry in this is SPTE contains info about the current status of the page.

when page fault occur, system will first look up SPT to ensure if the page is 
valid and where to load from. If ON_FRAME, then it is accessible. If ON_SWAP,
then use vm_swap_in to reload it. If ON_FILESYS, it is reloaded from the executable 
or data file. If not in SPT, it is invalid access or valid stack grow request.

when new page mapped into memory , a corresponding SPTE will be created to track
its location. if process write to a page, its state and metadata are updated to 
ensure consistency among virtual memory, frame tables, and swap disks.

when page need to evicte, the clock algo will choose a page based on its history
and update SPTE to eflect whether the page was swapped out or discarded.

>> A3: How does your code coordinate accessed and dirty bits between
>> kernel and user virtual addresses that alias a single frame, or
>> alternatively how do you avoid the issue?

The access bit and dirty bit are important for ensuring data consistency 
between user processes and the kernel.
we know that multiple virtual address can map to the same frame, thus we
need to manage these bits to prevent unnecessary disk writing and evicting.

when using page, its access bit will set. if the page's bit is still not set
after second check, it will be inactive or be evicted.

dirty bit indicates whether the page is modified.Before evicting, the dirty
page must be written back to swap space or disk, and the clean page can be 
safely discarded.The system checks pagedir_is_dirty and spte->dirty to decide.

to prevent the race condition, vm_frame_pin / vm_frame_unpin ensures that 
pages used for I/O or memory-mapped files are not evicted prematurely. 
after the operation, they can be swapped in as needed.

---- SYNCHRONIZATION ----

>> A4: When two user processes both need a new frame at the same time,
>> how are races avoided?

frame_lock use to avoid race conditions if two user processes request a new 
frame at the same time. this lock ensure only one process can modify the frame 
table at a time, preventing inconsistencies.

If no free frames, system invokes the eviction, using a clock algorithm to select
a victim frame. The frame is checked to ensure it is not pinned in current. 
If frame is chosen for eviction, the system updates SPT and swap space before 
releasing the frame for reusing.

Also, when a process requests a frame, the frame is temporarily pinned to prevent it 
from being evicted when initialization. After complete initializing, the frame is unpinned 
so that the eviction algo can consider it when needed.

---- RATIONALE ----

>> A5: Why did you choose the data structure(s) that you did for
>> representing virtual-to-physical mappings?

To achieve efficient, fast to look up, and properly synchronized, 
i use hash tables as SPTs, allowing for fast search for virtual page and status. 
this is improtanr for handling page faults and manage pages, whcih are swapped out or 
mapped from files. The frame table is also managed using hash tables to make fast access 
to physical frames, and uses linked lists to support an eviction clock algo. The 
clock algo helps identify pages and can be safely evicted without frequent scanning.

in swap management, i use a bitmap which can efficiently track free and used swap slots with 
minimal overhead. this makes O(1) allocation and deallocation in swap space. and aslo ensure swap 
operations can proceed smoothly without fragmentation. Also, synchronization is handled 
with a frame_lock can prevent race conditions.The system also has a pinning mechanism 
which prevent frame eviction adn  reducing risk of data corruption.

my data structures can balance performance, memory efficiency, and concurrency control. 
The combination of hash tables for fast search and check, linked lists for eviction, bitmaps 
for swap space, and locks for synchronization. all or this ensures that the system can handle virtual 
memory operations efficiently when prevent conflicts and unnecessary disk writes.

