>> B2: When a frame is required but none is free, some frame must be
>> evicted.  Describe your code for choosing a frame to evict.

if process needs a new frame but no free frame are available, 
the system will use clock algo to evict existing frames. 
It keeps a list of frames and a pointer which can move between 
them. each frame has an access bit and when it set, indicate frame 
has been used recently. thus the system clears the bit and moves on. 
If access bit is clear, the frame will be evicted soon.

Before evicting, the system checks if dirty bit is set. 
If so, it will saves the page to swap space or its original file 
before evicting it. otherwise, it is removed immediately. 
system updates the page table to show page is swapped out and 
clears the frame mapping in the process's page directory.

finally, the evicted frame will in requesting process, and 
the required page will loaded from swap, a file, or created as an empty page. 
This method will frequently check which page is used and unused, 
thereby improving system performance.

>> B3: When a process P obtains a frame that was previously used by a
>> process Q, how do you adjust the page table (and any other data
>> structures) to reflect the frame Q no longer has?

when process P takes over a frame whcih is previously used by process Q, 
the system check Q hasn't access by updating its page tables and related struct. 
first, the system use pagedir_clear_page to clear Q's page table entry. 
then, if the page swapped out, the swap index is recorded in Q's SPT 
then page can be retrieved later. 

If the page mapped from a file, it is written back before deleting. Then, 
the frame table entry updated and reflect P can own the frame linked 
to P's virtual page. Finally, the frame is prepared for P by loading its 
requested data as a zero-filled page. This make sure Q loses access 
to the frame and P can receive a clean and correctly mapped region of memory.

>> B4: Explain your heuristic for deciding whether a page fault for an
>> invalid virtual address should cause the stack to be extended into
>> the page that faulted.

when a page fault occur becasue an invalid virtual address, the system 
should decide if grow the stack or terminate the process. The 
stack growth heuristic will check if the error address is within a proper 
range of the current stack pointer. if the address below the current 
stack pointer but within a few bytes, then it is a valid stack growth 
case. If met these conditions, the system allocates a new page and maps it on
the process' stack. otherwise, the process will terminate. this ensures 
controlled stack growth and allow program execution and prevent arbitrary memory access.

