>> B5: Explain the basics of your VM synchronization design.  In
>> particular, explain how it prevents deadlock.  (Refer to the
>> textbook for an explanation of the necessary conditions for
>> deadlock.)

virtual memory system use locks and semaphores to synchronize 
access of shared resources. because of it, multiple processes can 
allocate, evict, and swap frames without conflict. in the meanwhile, 
the frame table, swap space can protected by synchronization 
mechanisms and prevent race conditions.

to avoid deadlock, the system follows lock ordering strategy. 
for example, when a process requires a new frame, it will
locks the frame table before it modify the page table or swap space. 
alos, evictions will occur in using a clock algo to make sure that 
frames are unlocked. all of this will before eviction and the 
eviction process completes also need to before another process 
attempts to use the same frame.

locking is another key mechanism to prevent race conditions. When a 
frame is actively in use, it is locked. and prevent eviction until 
the operation is complete. this can avoid the sityaion of one process 
from evicting a frame which is in use by another process.

