>> B7: Suppose a page fault in process P causes a page to be read from
>> the file system or swap.  How do you ensure that a second process Q
>> cannot interfere by e.g. attempting to evict the frame while it is
>> still being read in?

when process P loads a page from the file system or swap, i pin the 
frame. in this way, process Q can’t evict it becasue it’s being read. 
the system will keep only one process accesses a swap slot or file 
data at a time. the page table alos update only after loading is 
complete. thus, if Q tries to access it early, it triggers another page 
fault and waits. once the page finish loaded, i unpin the frame and make 
it available again. the above can avoid Q from interfering and keeps 
the data safe.

>> B8: Explain how you handle access to paged-out pages that occur
>> during system calls.  Do you use page faults to bring in pages (as
>> in user programs), or do you have a mechanism for "locking" frames
>> into physical memory, or do you use some other design?  How do you
>> gracefully handle attempted accesses to invalid virtual addresses?

during system call, if the process tries to access a page whic paged out,
page fault occurs and i use the SPT to check if page exists swap or file system. 
If so, i reload the page into memory and update the page table. 
to avoid conflicts, i pin the frame during loading. thus it is not evicted midway 
through the operation.

for system calls involving user memory access, i verify that the address 
is valid and belongs to the process. if ninvalid address, i handle 
it gracefully by terminating the process instead of letting it cause 
undefined behavior.

this approach can let system calls safely access user memory and keep memory 
management efficient and safe.

