>> C3: Explain how you determine whether a new file mapping overlaps
>> any existing segment.

when creating a new memory-mapped file, the system wiil first checks 
if it overlaps with any existing mapped regions. each process keeps a 
list of mmap_list, which stores info like the start address, size, and file.

to check for overlap, the system loops through this list and compares 
the new mapping’s address range with existing ones. it’s considered an 
overlap or request is rejected if the new mapping’s start or end falls 
inside an already mapped region. if there’s no conflict, 
the system adds it to the list and updates the page table.

---- RATIONALE ----

>> C4: Mappings created with "mmap" have similar semantics to those of
>> data demand-paged from executables, except that "mmap" mappings are
>> written back to their original files, not to swap.  This implies
>> that much of their implementation can be shared.  Explain why your
>> implementation either does or does not share much of the code for
>> the two situations.

my implementation shares a lot of code between demand-paged executable
data and memory-mapped files. since both involve lazily loading pages into 
memory when they are accessed. the key difference is where the page is 
written back when it is evicted. pages loaded from executable files do 
not need to be written back and memory-mapped file pages are written 
back to their original file, not swapped out.

to handle this efficiently, SPT keeps track f whether a page came from a 
file or swapped. during a page fault, include types of pages are loaded 
using similar logic. check the SPT, find a free frame, and bring the data 
into memory. however, for evictions, it is written back to its mapped file 
if a memory-mapped page is dirty. also executable pages are 
simply discarded and can be reloaded from the binary when needed.

this design allows me to reuse much of the page fault. alos can use frame allocation 
logic when making minimal changes to handle the differences in eviction behavior. 
it simplifies the implementation and ensure that both types of pages are 
managed correctly.

